use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Dragging import Dragging

export ui-component ScrollArea:
    description:
        A scroll area is a region whose content scrolls, with scrollbars drawn
        to match the rest of the design instead of the platform's own. The
        scrolling itself is still the platform's, so it feels and works the
        same as anywhere else.

    behaviors:
        - @{Dragging}

    nativeScrolling:
        Every usual way of scrolling keeps working: mouse wheel, trackpad,
        touch with its momentum, keyboard, scrolling to whatever gets focus,
        and finding text on the page. Assistive tools see ordinary scrolling
        content.

    scrollbars:
        It can scroll up and down, across, or both. Each scrollbar has a thumb
        whose size shows how much of the content is in view, and whose place
        shows where the view is. Where two scrollbars meet, a corner fills the
        gap.

    visibility:
        A scrollbar only appears when there is more content than fits that
        way. The component decides whether it then stays visible, or only
        shows while the pointer is over the area or while it is scrolling,
        fading out afterwards.

    pointer:
        The user can drag a thumb to scroll. What clicking the track beside the
        thumb does, such as jumping there or moving a page at a time, is up to
        the chosen library.

    keyboard:
        When the content doesn't fit and holds nothing that can take focus,
        the area itself can take focus, so keyboard users can scroll it with
        the arrow and page keys. The drawn scrollbars never take focus, and
        assistive tools ignore them.

    edges:
        The component can tell whether the content can scroll further in each
        direction, so it can show a hint such as a fading edge. It can also
        scroll the area to an edge or a given place.

    direction:
        For right-to-left languages, content scrolls from the right. Which side
        the up-and-down scrollbar sits on then is up to the chosen library.

    nonGoals:
        - Doesn't change how scrolling feels. No smoothing, snapping, or
            custom momentum beyond the platform's own
        - Doesn't draw only what's on screen. That's Virtualisation
        - No loading more content when the user reaches the end
